[Music]  oh there we go welcome back to las vegas  now here in this week's la vegas now  segment  our expert eric palacios breaks down  what to do when stopped by police is an  extremely important and timely reminder  take a look  eric welcome back thanks for having me  all right so we're back here again  talking about um what to do when you're  pulled over because unfortunately  you know especially people within the  black community are still so afraid  um of being approached or pulled over by  the police so let's just talk about  what do you do when you're pulled over  it's very important to be respectful and  to not do  anything to make the officers  uh suspicious or uh to think that you're  going to try to flee  or or do something that's a danger to  them so it's always important  if you see sirens and lights you're  being pulled over turn your signal  on your turn signal that way the police  knows he saw me he acknowledges he's  going to pull over as soon as it's okay  to do so when you pull over make sure  it's  hopefully in an area that's well lit and  safe uh turn off the car uh  and put your hands on the steering wheel  uh it's also important to lower your  window about halfway  that allows the officer to talk to you  but it does not allow him to focus head  in or smell  uh what's in your car or to do an  inspection uh which he  should not do okay that's a good  reminder um and  you know sometimes things escalate when  the officer starts asking questions so  do you have to answer the questions  that's a great question you do not the  fifth amendment  uh gives you uh that privilege you don't  have to  talk you don't have to incriminate  yourself you don't have to answer any  questions  that being said most officers are  trained to  just ask rudimentary background  questions  uh where are you going where are you  coming from  do you live close to here who's in the  car with you have you been drinking  uh do you have anything illegal in the  car uh  most of us